 Do not force an abstraction. Prefer boring code if the current shape is already clear, local, and unlikely to grow. Be skeptical of an abstraction that adds indirection without removing branches, duplicated rules, invalid states, or lifecycle risk.
 
 The sign that you skipped this is a new feature that grows an existing if/else chain by one more branch, or a second boolean that must stay in sync with the first. Temporal decomposition is another sign. Phase-named modules repeat the same domain rules across steps.
+
+**One name per concept.** When the code already names a concept, keep that name. Do not add a second word for the same concept beside the first. When the user's word differs from the code's, use the code's word and tell the user which word the code uses. In review, flag a new name for a concept the code already names.
+
+**Separate contexts.** One word can name different concepts in two parts of the codebase. Keep a separate type in each part. Do not combine them into one type whose fields are optional per meaning. Share only what means the same thing in both, such as an id. Translate at the one place where the parts exchange data.
+
+**Shared code answers to every caller.** Before you change a shared function, type, or constant for one request, list its other callers. When a caller serves a different business function, keep its behavior. Fork the shared piece and change only the requester's path. Look-alike code that different business functions change is two decisions. Keep the copies apart even when they match today.
